      "text": "The original height of the cone is 30 inches and the circumference of the base is \\(C = 16\\pi\\) inches. The radius \\(r\\) of the base can be calculated using the formula \\( C = 2\\pi r \\):\n\n\\[\n16\\pi = 2\\pi r \\implies r = 8 \\text{ inches}\n\\]\n\nNext, we can use the formula for the volume \\(V\\) of a cone:\n\n\\[\nV = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi r^2 h\n\\]\n\nOnce we know the height \\(h = 30\\) inches, we can substitute the values into the volume formula:\n\n\\[\nV = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi (8)^2 (30) = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi (64) (30) = 640\\pi \\text{ cubic inches}\n\\]\n\nNext, let's denote the height of the shorter cone as \\(h'\\) inches, and since the circumference remains the same:\n\n\\[\nC = 2\\pi (r) = 2\\pi (8) = 16\\pi\n\\]\n\nSince the circumference of the base changes but the radius remains 8 inches (same as the original cone), the radius stays the same. To find \\(h'\\):\n\n\\[\nh' = \\frac {V}{\\frac {1}{3} \\pi (8)^2}\n\\]\n\nThe volume of the shorter cone is given as \\(192\\pi\\) cubic inches, and we know \\(V = 640\\pi\\). Substitute this value:\n\n\\[\n192\\pi = \\frac {640\\pi}{\\frac {1}{3} \\pi (8)^2}\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(h'\\):\n\n\\[\n192\\pi h' = 640\\pi \\implies h' = 640 \\text{ inches}\n\\]\n\nSince we know the original height \\(h = 30\\) inches:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{h'}{h} = \\frac{640}{30} = \\frac{64}{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the ratio of the shorter height to the original height is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{64}{3}}\\). Let's double-check our calculations using Python code.\n```python\n# Constants\r\nC_original = 16 * 3.1415  # Circumference in inches\r\nradius_original = C_original / (2 * 3.1415)  # Radius in inches\r\nheight_original = 30  # Original height in inches\r\nvolume_shorter = 192 * 3.1415  # Volume of shorter cone in cubic inches\r\n\r\n# Volume of the original cone\r\nvolume_original = (1/3) * 3.1415 * (radius_original ** 2) * height_original\r\n\r\n# Solving for the height of the shorter cone using the volume formula\r\nheight_shorter = volume_shorter / ((1/3) * 3.1415 * (radius_original ** 2))\r\nratio = height_shorter / height_original\r\n\r\n# Convert ratio to the desired common fraction\r\nimport fractions\r\nratio_fraction = fractions.Fraction(height_shorter / height_original).limit_denominator()\r\n\r\nprint(((height_shorter, ratio, ratio_fraction)))\n```",
